[{:seon.task/name "avet-seek-allocates"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.db/eid` (one `(d/datoms db :avet a v)` seek, the path of `datahike.db.utils/entid`) costs 13-17 us and 23-32 KB at 100-10k tasks, growing with log n. The work is one seek through 2-3 B-tree nodes of ~6 comparisons each, answering one datom: ~3 us and ~1.3 KB. The cause is an allocation in our Datahike fork (reference-code/datahike: `datahike.db.utils`, `datahike.index`) or our persistent-sorted-set port (reference-code/persistent-sorted-set) on Jolt: find it with seon.profile and `seon.cost/measure`, and fix it there on a jolt/<topic> branch. Correct: the proof green with its :overrun key deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-small-transaction-costs-its-datoms"
  :seon.task/plan "`d/with` of one 5-datom definition record costs 221-233 us and 148-162 KB, flat in program size (100-10k vars). The work is ~15 index inserts at ~1.3 us (`seon.cost/units` :sorted/assoc), 20-30 us: ~10x over. The cause is Datahike's per-transaction overhead on Jolt (tx report, schema checks, index wrappers): find it with seon.profile and fix it in our fork. Correct: the cost proof green at its estimate."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/a-definition-record-costs-its-datoms}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-transaction-costs-its-attributes"
  :seon.task/plan "The one schema transaction (`seon.db/schema`, 129 attributes, 407 datoms) costs 45-49 ms in memory (`d/db-with` on an empty db; half the schema 15.5-16.7 ms: superlinear), against 0.12 ms for a 2-datom note. Cause, in our Datahike: `update-rschema` (reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc:148) recomputes the whole rschema (`dbu/rschema`) after every schema datom, so n attributes cost O(n x attributes). Correct, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Datahike: the rschema derived once per transaction, after its last schema datom (or updated by the one attribute that changed). Estimate: ~407 datom inserts at ~1.3 us plus one rschema derivation over 129 attributes (~50 us), about 1 ms. The schema transaction runs once per store (a check run, the test store), not per boot: it prices every store's creation and any schema change."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-cost-test/a-schema-transaction-is-linear-in-its-attributes}}
 {:seon.task/name "two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Since task each-id-is-issued-once (worktree session global-ids, 2026-10-23) entity ids and transaction ids come from one page every process shares (seon.db/ids!), reseeded from the heads at open: of what follows, only the roster append remains. Several processes writing different branches of one store is safe and is the design: each commits its own branch (content-addressed immutable node keys plus its one head key, each put one LMDB write transaction; konserve.jolt.lmdb:86). Changing the roster is not: `d/branch!` (`datahike.versioning/branch!`) appends the :branches log with `konserve.core/append` (reference-code/konserve src/konserve/core.cljc:570), which reads the log head and then makes three writes (the element, the old last element's :next, the head) under go-locked, a lock of this process only (konserve.jolt.lmdb's blob lock is a no-op token, lmdb.jolt:28). Two processes branching at once can each link after the same last element, and one branch drops out of `d/branches` (its head key stays, so `seon.db/branch?` still finds it); a process SIGKILLed between the writes leaves the same tear. The entity-id counter is per process too: each root reserves its agents' blocks from its own counter (`seon.db/lease-block!`), seeded once past every branch head then committed, so two roots on one store hand out overlapping blocks; that is harmless only while their mains never merge into each other. Production has one root per store, the only process that branches (the writer's :spawn and :clone-branch); several roots on one store (parallel check steps as separate roots, a standing bin/root beside a T5 run on <SEON_RUNS>/store) meet both. Correct: either the roster append is one LMDB write transaction (a konserve.jolt.lmdb -update-in over the three keys in one txn, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our konserve), or one root per store at a time is a failure point with its edge (`seon.db/open!` refusing a store another live root holds, e.g. by an flock on <store>/root.lock). Parallel checks share one root, each step on its own branch (production's shape), with the crash steps (durable, window, resume, orphan) run with nothing else on the store."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-kept-store-gains-new-attributes-at-open"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.db/open!` installs the attributes a kept store lacks (`seon.db/install-missing!`, one transaction, nothing when nothing is missing; Datahike accepts new attribute definitions at any time), and `seon.root/start!` ends the boot on a refused boot fact instead of ignoring the answer. Before, open! transacted `seon.db/schema` only when it seeded a new store, so a kept store refused root's boot fact (`seon.task/boot-tx` with `seon.config/facts`) for an attribute added later, start! ignored the reason, `seon.task/root` answered nil and no task could start. Not covered: an attribute whose definition changed (type, cardinality, uniqueness)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/opening-a-kept-store-installs-the-attributes-it-lacks}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-store-opens-where-it-lies"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.db/store-cfg` reads the id the store records (`seon.db/stored-id`: the :db head's stored config through Datahike's own read path, `datahike.writing/read-head`, 0.8 ms), and mints `(hasch/uuid path)` only for a store it is about to make, so a renamed or copied store opens at its new path. Before, the id was minted from the path given, and Datahike's connect refused a moved store (`datahike.connector/ensure-stored-config-consistency`, :store-identity-mismatch). A copy keeps its original's id, as a Datahike replica does (`datahike.store/physical-store-key`), so one process opens one of them. Left: `(:config @conn)` still names the path the store was made at (task deref-keeps-the-connections-config)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "deref-keeps-the-connections-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Done, in our Datahike at pin fd0c065 (jolt/deref-config): `datahike.connector/deref-conn` builds the db with the connection's own config, `(dsi/stored->db (assoc stored :config (:config @wrapped-atom)) store)`, as `datahike.connector/-connect-impl*` does, so after a store is moved `(:config @conn)` names its new path and a commit writes the new :store into the head. Before, with a :sync writer it answered the config stored at the head, the path the store was made at. Proof: a moved store's `(get-in @conn [:config :store :path])` is its new path."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes` is 64 GiB, raised from 1 GiB after two agents died on Datahike's {:type :fatal-commit-error} without confirming MDB_MAP_FULL (a failing commit's cause is now recorded, task seon.agent/a-death-names-its-cause: confirm it); checks and tests delete the branches they made (`seon.db/delete-main!`), and nothing reclaims them. A turn records its basis, not its context text (`:seon.turn/basis`, ~350 bytes, and `:seon.turn/evals`; `seon.context/render` remakes the exact text on that commit). Reproduction: the standing store held 1,305 MB of data.mdb over 278 branches under 68 run mains. test/seon/bench_commit.clj (fresh LMDB stores, bf 64): `d/branch!` writes 3 keys, 382 bytes; a 1-datom commit 11-14 keys, 16.8-27.7 KB; 100 evals committed one by one 4.9-6.9 MB (200-byte values) against 157-546 KB in one commit (26-35x); 50 per-turn commits 16.0 MB with the basis record (24.4 MB at an 8 KB context and 61.3 MB at 32 KB with the context text). `seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes` is red: 20 per-turn commits wrote 1.63 MB against a bound of 0.84 MB (the one commit plus 32 KB of path per commit). Cause: Datahike holds every value inline in its index leaves (twice, :eavt and :aevt), appends go into the tail leaf, and a copy-on-write commit rewrites that whole leaf, so a commit costs about bf/2 x the values already in the tail leaf, not its own size; the remaining gap is values still inline (the reply, reasoning, evals and the eval record's printed value). Branching factor is not the fix (bf 16: 1.5x less). Correct, in order: (1) values over a threshold held out of the index leaves, written once under their own content-addressed key with the datom carrying the reference, in our Datahike fork's node codec so every reader sees plain values (Postgres TOAST; emulated, 50 per-turn commits write ~2.0 MB of index plus each value once); a dependency and format change, root's go first; (2) reclaim: GC keeping only what live branch heads reach, then compaction, as an :io proc when a measured dead/live ratio passes a config key or on demand (DECISIONS: Storage reclaim), through Datahike's and konserve's own mechanisms (reference-code/datahike online-gc), never by deleting a branch, and keeping the commits turns name or past contexts become unreadable; `(datahike.api/gc-storage conn)` on Jolt throws (no dependency provides java.time.Instant), so that class is provided first (Jolt's RFC 0014 `:jolt/provides`) or the GC takes a path without it; (3) the map size set from the measured bound, with that provenance. v1 staged a value over 4,096 bytes out of line (`seon.blob/inline-or-stage`, ~/src/seon)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-entity-id-lookup"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.db/eid` is the one entity id lookup of a unique attribute's value (one AVET seek); the private copies (seon.task, seon.program) and the inline `(:e (first (d/datoms db :avet ...)))` in seon.merge, seon.source, seon.ref and my.schema are gone. Root's facts are read by a pull on the lookup ref `[:seon.root/id :root]` with no eid guard in front (a pull by lookup ref answers nil when the entity does not exist), `seon.config/from-db` and `seon.db/collect!` included. seon.db owns it because it owns the store and its schema, and every caller can require it. docs/storage.md names `seon.db/eid` as the one entity lookup."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/eid-is-the-entity-of-a-unique-value seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek seon.config-test/root-writes-the-config-on-main-and-the-db-answers-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-store-leaves-src"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: the test store helpers (test-store, test-db, test-branch!) live in test/seon/test_store.clj (`seon.test-store`, no _test suffix, so bin/test runs no tests of it), not in src; `seon.db/seed!` and `seon.db/open-main!` are public, the one way a store is seeded and a main opened. seon.fixtures/two-calls stays in src: seon.model resolves the scripted models by name in the built binary's root (seon.agent-test/in-agent imports only src), where a test namespace is not loaded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-test-takes-a-branch-of-the-test-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "dead-readers-are-cleared-at-open"
  :seon.task/plan "LMDB reuses a freed page only when no reader's snapshot is older than the txn that freed it, and a SIGKILLed process's reader slot pins its snapshot until mdb_reader_check clears it; nothing in src calls `konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi/reader-check`. Reproduction (jolt/runner e0efb69): four check steps at once, each its own root and agents on one LMDB store, filled the map: mdb_stat showed 65,534 of 65,536 pages used, 60,055 of them free (live data ~85 MB). Correct: clear dead readers where a process opens the env (root boot, agent open: one call), and keep read txns short. The map's size is `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes`; the store's growth is measured by task the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-dead-readers-slot-does-not-pin-freed-pages}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-commit-names-its-runtime"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: every transaction Seon makes carries the string attribute `:seon.tx/runtime` in its tx-meta (`seon.db/tx-meta`; one ~90 B datom, inline): the writer's group commit (`seon.writer` commit-batch's arg maps), an agent's `seon.agent/transact!` (`dw/apply!`), and `seon.db/open!`'s seed and install; the fork server applies main's tx-meta to its replica (`seon.db/report->tx-meta`) (DECISIONS: One store holds the whole system). Its value is `jolt.host/runtime-fingerprint` (jolt/runtime-fingerprint, 8cdbb687): Jolt's own compiled-unit key, `<jolt-version>-<runtime fingerprint>[-tr]` (reference-code/jolt host/chez/loader.ss aot-generation-dir; the fingerprint is baked over a binary's whole image and Chez's boot files, build.ss:2982-3022), which tells two dirty builds apart where `jolt.host/jolt-version` and `jolt.image/runtime-version` do not. Design and measurements: next/notes/archive/store-native-2026-10-22.md#q4. Replaces nothing; adds one attribute."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-writers-transactions-name-their-runtime
                      seon.procs-test/a-writers-merge-names-its-runtime
                      seon.agent-test/an-agents-transactions-name-their-runtime
                      seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base
                      seon.db-test/a-kept-store-from-before-the-runtime-attribute-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "stored-attributes-take-the-new-words"
  :seon.task/plan "Pass 2 of the rename (owner, 2026-10-22, DECISIONS \"Names are Clojure's\"; AGENTS.md section 7a; pass 1 is seon.docs/names-are-clojures-words), with root's rulings of 2026-10-22 (one word never names two causes):
- stuck: not renamed. Owner, 2026-10-23: \"no automatic retries, send it to root to decide\"; task hold-for-root deletes the refinement chain and with it :seon.task/stuck, seon.reconcile/stuck-tx and the problem class :stuck (an attempt refused is held for root as my.task/fail! holds a task). seon.reconcile/stuck, which reports every root cause of released work not done (a cancel, a hold), is seon.reconcile/root-causes with rows :seon.reconcile/root-cause and the quiescence report's :root-causes.
- waste stays: \"overrun\" is seon.cost's word for an estimate exceeded, another cause. The owner picks a word (suggested: :seon.attempt/misdirected, turns spent on what the agent was shown wrongly).
- refusal (not stored): the done rule's reasons, as m/explain's errors are: seon.reconcile/done-refusals -> done-explain, refusals-text -> explain-text, :seon.reconcile/refusal -> :seon.reconcile/not-done with its :refusal key -> :reason; the one error map: seon.contract/refusal -> invalid, seon.config/refusal -> invalid, seon.task's file-refusal -> file-refused, seon.writer's merge-refusal -> merge-refused, seon.schema.datahike's {:refusal ...} -> {:invalid ...}, :seon.print/requery-refusal -> requery-refused; prose says reason, error or refused.
- config keys, stored as facts on main (seon.config/facts): :seon.config.contract/guarantees -> instrument (SEON_INSTRUMENT), :seon.ai.model/thinking-dials -> thinking-modes, :seon.config.root/entrance-port -> server-port (SEON_SERVER_PORT). A checkout's config/local.edn holds the port key, so bin/ports owns its port keys and writes them on every run under their current names, keeping the ports and every other entry; bin/build, bin/test and bin/check run `bin/ports --write` first (their config reads the file; written only when it changes).
- task ids stay: an id is a record (embedded in its attempts' ids, branch names and turn bases), so seon.fences, seon.entrance and the tasks named with an old word keep their ids, as branch names do (jolt/land4).
No migration (owner, 2026-10-23: \"why are you migrating 6 facts? isn't it just test data just nuke it and move on\"): the standing store is erased when this merges and its root starts fresh under the new names. Datahike renames no attribute anyway (datahike.schema/find-invalid-schema-updates refuses a :db/ident change; no alias), so a kept store made before this holds its facts under the old names. Rehearsed before the ruling on a copy of next/runs/store: 6 facts on :main (4 thinking-dials, guarantees, entrance-port).
Done: the proof below green; bin/test green; bin/laws, bin/check mg-check, instrument and smoke OK; one full bin/test --all and bin/check 100."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/names-are-clojures-words"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-its-keys-under-their-current-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-id-is-issued-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: global addressing everywhere, so data is queryable across branches with d/q, d/pull and d/entity (notes/cross-branch-attack-2026-10-23.md). Entity ids came from per-agent leases of 100,000 (about 5,400 agents per store; a restarted root re-minted a block's unspent tail; two roots on one store overlapped) and transaction ids were per branch ((inc max-tx): two branches forked at one t gave their first transactions one id, measured in notes/global-ids/MEASURED.md §5). Now: one page of two 64-bit words beside the store (native/ids.c, seon.db/ids!), mapped by every process (forked children map it with no step), one atomic fetch-add per id (28-34 ns, 200-240 ns at 10 processes, §6); Datahike takes its ids through datahike.ids (jolt/global-ids: set-ids!, with-store-ids; a transaction takes its id when it begins and :max-tx is its branch's last); the page is moved past every head at open. Deleted: seon.db/lease!, lease-block!, lease-spent, lease-use, :seon.config.store/eid-lease, :seon.db/lease-spent and the :unrecorded path, Datahike's eid ceilings, bin/check lease; the fork server's replica replays main's commit as main's transaction (seon.db/replay). docs/storage.md#ids."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/each-id-is-issued-once-across-branches seon.db-test/a-reopened-store-issues-past-every-head seon.procs-test/the-fork-server-step}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-identity-names-its-entity"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner's ruling 2026-10-23 (notes/content-eids-2026-10-23.md, the pricing study): two branches minting one identity (a new namespace, a form, a var) made two entities, and a verbatim merge of the second was refused by uniqueness. Now an entity with a program identity takes the hash of [attribute value] as its id (Datahike jolt/global-ids: :db/contentId, content-eid, content-tempids; Seon: {:seon.db/content-id true} on :form/id :var/name :ns/name :method/key :impl/key :schema/key :schema.file/path), in [2^40, 2^60) beside the counter's ranges; an identity is never replaced on its entity (:entity-id/collision). :form/id is hasch's uuid of namespace and text (it was Clojure's 32-bit hash). Measured: the hash 17-19 us a call on Jolt; nodes written per commit 0.97x / 1.51x / 4.50x the counter's at 1 / 2 / 10 new identities (110k datoms), within the study's 1.3-5x model. docs/storage.md#ids."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/an-identity-names-its-entity-on-every-branch seon.db-test/content-ids-cost-the-writes-the-model-says
                      seon.merge-test/identical-defs-on-two-branches-merge-clean}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-collection-runs-beside-live-agents"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root collects the store only at quiescence with no agent live: `seon.reconcile/quiet` asks once per quiet spell, root's api refuses :collect while an agent is live, and the writer runs `seon.db/collect!` (docs/storage.md#collection). A commit can reuse a value out of line that a concurrent sweep would take, so on a busy root garbage from deleted branches waits for a quiet spell. Correct (the design in docs/storage.md#collection): the mark runs outside LMDB's writer lock; a short delta mark and the sweep run inside one write transaction; a commit that reuses a value re-asserts it in its own transaction. Deletes the api's no-agent-live reason of :collect."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collection-beside-a-committing-agent-keeps-every-value-it-reuses}}
 {:seon.task/name "values-compress-with-a-trained-dictionary"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every stored value is compressed by the codec `:seon.config.store/compressor` names (zstd level 3, one frame per value, `konserve.jolt.zstd`), each value's header naming its codec (docs/storage.md#compression). Measured there: a dictionary trained on our own values compresses small ones 2-5x better, and faster; it is unused because it needs a corpus that represents the running system. Correct: a dictionary trained (zstd's ZDICT_trainFromBuffer) on a kept store's values, stored in the store under its digest; a value's header names the dictionary, so a store holding values written with and without one reads both; retraining adds a dictionary and never rewrites old values. A konserve fork change on a jolt/<topic> branch, root's go first. Measure bytes per per-turn commit first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-value-written-with-a-dictionary-reads-back seon.db-test/a-dictionary-shrinks-what-a-commit-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-deleted-store-leaves-no-semaphore-set"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the robust LMDB build keys each store's SysV semaphore set by ftok(lock.mdb,'M') (mdb.c:6098, low 16 bits of the inode) and removes it only when the last process closes the env holding the exclusive lock (IPC_RMID). Check and test stores are deleted with their set still there. Reproduction: `ipcs -s | grep -c -- --ra-ra-r--` went from 912 to 4,352 in a day; kern.sysv.semmns is 87,381 (about 43k sets). Cause: nothing removes a store's set when the store is deleted. Effects: at the limit every new store's `seon.db/open!` fails in semget, and a key shared with a live store resets that store's writer lock (semctl SETALL). Correct: where a store directory is deleted, read `mti_semid` from its lock file (a konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi helper, a jolt/<topic> change with root's go) and IPC_RMID it. Fewer stores (`seon.check/branches-as-in-production`) slow the growth but do not end it. Deletes: nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-deleted-store-leaves-no-semaphore-set}}]
